   "why": "Jason Mount asked at 19:21 on 27/07 whether removal of the existing windows is allowed for. Adam answered direct at 19:56: 'I can confirm we have allowed for strip out of old frames. We have not allowed for disposal, ie skips on site.' WHY HE ASKED, which nobody had established: the client's own bill C0234 has a Collection page carrying three lines - General, STRIP OUT, and New windows and Doors. Our pricing document has no strip-out line at all. He was reconciling our number against a schedule with a strip-out line in it and finding nothing against it. THE COMMITMENT IS IN NEITHER DOCUMENT WE ISSUED - swept the proposal's 10 pages and the workbook's only sheet. AND IT IS NOT IN THE MONEY, WHICH IS NOW PROVEN RATHER THAN INFERRED. GBP 39,680 is exactly the sum of the house labour codes over 217 units, zero residual. The control settles what that means: the SAME codes give GBP 9,570 over 37 units on BROCKS HILL, a NEW TEACHING BLOCK with no existing window to remove, and GBP 8,500 over 52 units on Crestwood Park. We charge the identical per-unit fit rate whether or not there is a frame to strip out, so the rate is fit-only and carries no strip-out increment anywhere in the GBP 279,244.69. AND THE BILL'S STRIP-OUT ITEM IS FAR MORE THAN OLD FRAMES: item A also requires making good facing brickwork and pointing with matching bricks and tinted cement mortar, cutting back plaster and render internally and making good with one coat gypsum, and any internal damage made good at the Contractor's expense; item B requires OUT-OF-SEQUENCE RETURN VISITS for the staircase windows at three levels, removed for general site access; item C makes us liable for water damage on openings left exposed. Making good brickwork and plaster is builder's work and return visits are a programme cost - neither is fit labour. There is no strip-out rate on file (one of the 21 zero categories), so what it should cost cannot be stated. Quantity for an RFQ: 217 units / 336.17 m2. DISPOSAL IS NOT ONE-WAY AND THE ANSWER TREATED IT AS IF IT WERE: bill B5 deems all items to include removal to a GUILDMORE skip at the front of the building, which supports Adam exactly; but B35 requires old windows taken to a trade waste recycling transfer station with documentation retained, and the RBKC ER defines 'Remove' as including 'dispose of unwanted materials'. Our own proposal meanwhile excludes waste removal generally, which disclaims even the carry-to-skip that B5 deems included.",
   "needs": "One decision - reissue or not - but the reissue now carries FOUR corrections rather than one, so it is worth more than it was yesterday. (1) STRIP-OUT stated in writing on OUR definition, removal of frames, before Guildmore's sub-contract order adopts bill item A's making-good wholesale. (2) MASTIC/EPDM wording (REQ-6). (3) THE SCAFFOLD SCHEDULE: bill B21 says any additional scaffolding must be DETAILED IN YOUR TENDER RETURN, that Guildmore will instruct the scaffolding company, and that anything not identified 'after commencement will be deducted from works value'. We returned a blanket Access/Lifting Equipment exclusion and identified nothing - which is the one response that clause is drafted to defeat, because it names no requirement for Guildmore to instruct. Still fixable before commencement. (4) THE INSURANCE-BACKED GUARANTEE: bill B72 requires a comprehensive insurance-backed minimum FENSA 10-year guarantee covering ALL units installed, with the premium paid and the policy document issued IN FAVOUR OF THE EMPLOYER BEFORE PRACTICAL COMPLETION. We offered the house self-backed 10-year warranty - no insurer, no premium, no policy, no FENSA registration and no start date. Second job this week after Lower Range Road, and unlike Lower Range this one is already with the client. NOTE THE CLOCK: Guildmore said on 06/07 they would issue a fully developed design in 4-6 weeks for a full and final offer, which lands mid-August. Everything here is worth fixing before that reprice rather than after.",
   "options": [
    "Reissue a corrected proposal - strip-out, mastic/EPDM, scaffold schedule and the guarantee in one pass",
    "Reissue for strip-out and mastic/EPDM only, carry the scaffold and guarantee to the mid-August reprice",
    "Leave it in the thread - record all four as known exposures and change nothing",
    "Price strip-out as a variation and put a figure to Guildmore"
   ],
   "status": "answered",
   "answer": "ANSWERED AND CLOSED - AND MY FRAMING OF IT WAS WRONG. Adam, by email 28/07 21:01 BST: \"Regarding the strip out, we had a lot across this job compared to the material costs. Therefore I decided I would include the strip out (effectively FOC) in order to remain competitive.\" And on the hub at 21:47 BST: \"I have already addressed this with you.\" The finding was right on the facts and wrong on the conclusion. There IS no strip-out money in the GBP 279,244.69 - GBP 39,680 is exactly the house labour codes over 217 units, and the same codes give GBP 9,570 on Brocks Hill, a new-build block with nothing to remove, so the rate is demonstrably fit-only. But the absence is a PRICED DECISION, not an oversight: Adam saw the strip-out content against the material cost and gave it away to stay competitive. His reply to Jason Mount was therefore accurate, and the margin carries it. Recorded as a decision. The one thing his answer does not settle is what the word covers - Guildmore's bill puts making good facing brickwork and pointing with matching bricks and tinted mortar, cutting back and making good plaster, and out-of-sequence return visits for the staircase windows inside their 'Strip out' line. Giving away frame removal is a different decision from giving away builder's work. NOT reopened: it bites at sub-contract order stage, not now, and it is recorded in data/jobs/princess-beatrice.md for whoever reads that order. TWO ITEMS BUNDLED INTO THIS REQUEST WERE NEVER ABOUT STRIP-OUT and are genuinely unaddressed - bill B21's scaffold-deduction clause against our blanket exclusion, and bill B72's insurance-backed FENSA guarantee premium against our self-backed warranty. Both were folded in here on 28/07 to avoid raising new requests against an already-full board, and that is why they got lost behind strip-out. Sent to Adam by email on his own instruction (\"if you believe there are any further issues, please email me and be concise\") rather than reopened here.",
   "answered_by": "Adam (email + hub, dashmsg-57)",
