     "basis": "Not a whole-job comparison - a BAND comparison, which is the useful kind. BSW quotation QT250834, 15/06/2026, quoted to Fenster for PRIDE DEVELOPMENTS' Severn Trent job: Sheerline Prestige aluminium casements, factory glazed, trickle vents, shootbolt locking, cills and couplers. Six lines, 27 units, 72.578 m2, Total Nett Ex VAT GBP 34,902.35, which the extracted lines reconcile to exactly. Unit rates GBP 629.63/m2 at 1.440 m2 down to GBP 336.78/m2 at 6.750 m2 fit rate = 721.47 x area^-0.4093 with R2 = 0.9934. Redditch's 43 items re-priced on that curve give GBP 56,995.38 of frame supply against the engine's GBP 60,551.26. Same supplier, same client, six weeks old, same product family, and the size range brackets Redditch's.",
     "lesson_followup_2026_07_29": "The last line of this lesson - that the code adder is a fixed sum per unit and so quietly halves margin on big units - turns out to be only half the story. The estimator had already noticed and had already been correcting for it by hand; nobody had written the correction down. Measured across all 30 sent pricing documents, the money in the unit rate over and above Frames is the code value x 75% almost exactly below 6 m2 (median x1.00 against the template adder, n=453 lines) and the code value x 125% above 6 m2 (median x1.250, n=60, nine different jobs). So the template's fixed adder is right where it was set, and wrong only past 6 m2, where the estimator silently switches to 125%. That is now in the engine as ADDER_FACTOR_LARGE - see the entry of 2026-07-29.",
     "lesson": "THE BAND STRUCTURE IS NOW EVIDENCED TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY, AND IT IS THE REAL FINDING - the -5.9% whole-job number is not. Engine error by band (positive = engine sits above the real price): under 1.5 m2, St Mary's -35.5% against Severn Trent -38.9%; 1.5-3 m2, -1.2% against -20.4%; 3-6 m2, +37.5% against +18.1%; over 6 m2, +35.2% against +34.1%. Two different jobs, different suppliers, different dates, agreeing within four points on the small band and within one point on the large band. The register UNDER-prices small units by roughly a third and OVER-prices large ones by roughly a third, and on a broad mix those errors cancel - which is exactly why whole-job accuracy has looked better than per-element accuracy all along. St Mary's said this from one job and it was reasonable to hold it lightly; it should not be held lightly any more. TWO CAVEATS THAT BOTH PUSH THE COMPARATOR HIGH, so the engine's over-pricing on the big bands is if anything understated here: the Severn Trent job is 3005 Wine Red metallic, a premium colour, and its outer pane is 6.8 laminated rather than 4mm toughened. AND A SEPARATE ONE WORTH ITS OWN LINE: the house template's code adder is a FIXED SUM PER UNIT, so margin falls as units grow - 50.7% of the frame line under 1.5 m2, 19.0% at 3-6 m2, 12.2% over 6 m2. Redditch averages 3.18 m2 and earns 24.7%; Crestwood Park averaged 1.29 m2 and earned 42.9%. Nobody had noticed that the template quietly prices big-unit work at half the margin of small-unit work.",
