   "body": "Adam,\n John North Hall, 1-39 Vaughan House - Works Order 701256543, tender back 9am Monday 24 August.\n I have read the ITT end to end, the estate plan, the SMA profile and all six images. I can't price it yet, and\nthe reason is worth two minutes of your time.\n THERE ARE NO DOOR SIZES IN THE PACK\nNot one. No door schedule, no elevation, no opening register, no drawing. The &quot;Block Plan&quot; is an estate plan of\nparking spaces and flat stacks. All we have is five photographs. Bellview cannot quote five doorsets without\nfive sizes, so the RFQ can't go out and there is no price.\n Jordan Jones has offered to meet us on site and that offer is the answer. It needs taking in the first week of\nAugust - survey, then RFQ, then about a week for Bellview, then price and check, against a 9am Monday deadline\nleaves less room than 27 days sounds. Worth knowing that clauses 1.4.2 and 3.1.2 both say no claim for extras\nwill be entertained for anything a site visit would have revealed, and 3.1.1 deems us to have visited whether\nwe did or not.\n SIGNING THEIR FORM OF TENDER DELETES OUR EXCLUSIONS\nSection 5.0, clause 4.1: &quot;any caveats, assumptions, reservations or exclusions that may be printed on\ncorrespondence emanating from the tender ... shall not be applicable to this tender or agreement.&quot; The clause\nabove it offers the works &quot;for the firm price contained within the pricing summary&quot;, and signing that page is\nhow you submit - there is no unsigned route.\n So our standard exclusions schedule does nothing here: access plant, waste removal, making good, access\ncontrol, all disapplied by our own signature. On this job an item is either priced, or accepted in writing\nbefore the 24th. There is no third option, and writing the exclusions more clearly doesn't help.\n That also settles the strip-out question for this job specifically. Your position to Jason Mount - strip out\nyes, disposal no - is the right house default, but this ITT puts removal AND disposal in our tender by name,\nasks for a Waste Carrier Licence with the submission, and allows skips in the compound. Both are ours here.\n THE INTERCOM CAN'T WORK ON THE HARDWARE THEY HAVE SPECIFIED\nThey require the intercoms reconnected and proven working on completion. Their own hardware list is a\nmechanical cylinder with a thumbturn and nothing else - no electric strike, no keep, no rectifier, no power\ntransfer. The entry panels are on the brickwork beside each door in all five photos, so they aren't moving; the\nwork is the electric release inside the new doorset, and it's a door-entry specialist's, not ours. Clause 2.1.4\nwants every subcontractor named with the tender, so one has to be chosen before we submit.\n TWO GATES I CAN'T CHECK\nClause 4.3.2 sets public liability at a minimum of GBP 10,000,000, and 2.5 wants a Waste Carrier Licence\nenclosed with the tender. Neither is recorded anywhere I can see. If we're short on either, the survey day is\nwasted, so it's worth confirming first.\n WHAT IT'S WORTH\nBenchmark only, and the size is assumed, so please don't quote it to anyone: GBP 24,000-26,000 for the five\ndoorsets - frames, glass and fit. That's anchored on Bellview's own Smart Wall Pocket prices, the closest being\nLyttleton Road, a 1600 x 2100 door with a fixed side field at GBP 2,930.12 net. Across the plausible range of\nsizes and rates it moves between GBP 21,700 and GBP 29,600.\n I'd expect that to be about half the tender. Strip-out and disposal, making good inside and out, the intercom\nwork, 129 keys, and the client's own Preliminaries and Contingency lines are all on top - and none of them has\na rate in our register, so they need real prices rather than an allowance. Their pricing summary has three\nlines and our pricing document answers one of them, which is the same thing that prompted Guildmore's question\nabout removal.\n ONE THING TO PUT TO JORDAN JONES WHATEVER WE DECIDE\nTheir ITT names the blocks 1-6, 7-16, 17-23, 24-31, 32-39. Their own estate plan and the nameplates in their\nown photographs both say 1-6, 7-15, 16-23, 24-31, 32-39, with no flat 13 on the estate. Their key count proves\nit: they ask for 129 keys on a stated basis of 3 per flat plus 3 per doorset, and 38 flats gives exactly 129\nwhere 39 would give 132. Flat 16 is served by the third door, not the second. It matters because the keys are\nfive differs - cutting them off their block ranges orders 33 and 24 where 27 and 27 are needed.\n Also worth flagging to them, since they specified the system themselves: SMA's own datasheet, which they sent\nus, publishes 1.8 W/m2K for Smart Wall doors. The ITT sets no U-value at all, and the limiting standard for a\nreplacement door under Part L is tighter than that. If there's a conflict it's inside their own specification,\nand they'd want to know before they consult the leaseholders on the price under Section 20, not after.\n WHAT I NEED FROM YOU\n1. Whether we're bidding it at all.\n2. If yes, the site survey booked with Jordan Jones - he needs the date and time in advance.\n3. Confirmation of the GBP 10m PL cover and the waste carrier licence.\n4. Your hourly rates for tradesmen and labourers, and the eight-trade daywork schedule at clause 2.6. Those are\nyour numbers rather than mine, and clause 2.1.3 makes them part of the contract and the basis for pricing\nevery variation for the life of the job.\n It's on the hub as REQ-32 with the options to click. Nothing has gone to the client and nothing will until you\nsay so.\n Mary\n Kind regards,\n Mary Grace \n Estimator\n 01908 429200 \n 97-98 Alston Drive\nBradwell Abbey, MK13 9HF \n www.fensterglazing.com \n\n This email and any attachments to it may be confidential and are intended solely for the use of the individual to whom it is addressed. Any views or opinions expressed are solely those of the author and do not necessarily represent those of Fenster Glazing & Locks Ltd. If you are not the intended recipient of this email, you must neither take any action based upon its contents, nor copy or s"
